## Diagnosis: tel-compared=0 on CLIENT

The counter stays at 0 across every heartbeat. Since the counter is cumulative
and never reset, this is NOT a cache-window miss — no teleport event ever
entered the client's _pendingTeleportEvent slot during the session.

Three candidate causes (no code change; user-side triage):

  (1) Client's fall-respawn never actually fired.
      The V5 R2 plan required client to fall off the track at least once.
      If the session never triggered a fall (user stayed on the track), the
      bridge is never called and the queue stays empty. Confirm by checking
      whether the client's Buddah visually respawned during the session.

  (2) Fall-respawn fired but routed through the LEGACY fallback.
      BuddahRespawn.cs:156 requires predictionBootstrap.IsPredictionModeActive()
      AND predictionRespawnBridge != null. If either fails, BuddahRespawn
      falls back to TeleportToWorldPose at line 168 which does direct
      rb.position = targetPosition — no queue event, shadow blind.
      Since imp-compared=2 proves prediction mode IS active on client (impulse
      path used the predicted-queue branch), the likely culprit inside this
      candidate is predictionRespawnBridge being null on the Buddah prefab
      instance. Requires inspecting the instantiated Buddah NetworkObject in
      the scene to confirm the BuddahPredictionRespawnBridge component is
      wired up with predictedMotor reference set.

  (3) Client fell BEFORE the first captured heartbeat (T=4690).
      Ruled out — cumulative counters are monotonic. If respawn had happened
      before T=4690, tel-compared would read >=1 from T=4690 onward. It reads
      0 at T=4690 and stays there.

Most likely (1) — user either didn't fall this session, or the fall-respawn
was triggered on the HOST's Buddah rather than the CLIENT's Buddah. Host log
will clarify: if host peer shows tel-compared >= 1, then the fall that
happened belonged to host's Buddah, not client's. V5 R2 would still need
client-side teleport coverage, so a targeted re-run with a client-fall is
needed.

## Next Step
Scrape HOST console next. If host shows tel-compared >= 1, the fall happened
on host's side — we need a follow-up micro-run where client specifically
falls off the track (host can stay idle or drive normally) so that
client-side tel-compared also reaches >= 1. If host ALSO shows tel-compared=0,
it's candidate (2) — the predictionRespawnBridge wiring needs inspection.

Per V5 protocol: no auto-fix. Report diagnosis and wait.
